<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Talimali Band of Apalachee</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Talimali Band of Apalachee is a community of descendants of the Apalachee people living in Rapides Parish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>. Their ancestors left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> after the destruction of the Spanish missions in 1704, settling near Mobile and later moving west into Louisiana in the 18th century. The band preserves its Apalachee identity and has sought official recognition as a tribe.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/talimali-band-of-apalachee.jpg" alt="Talimali Band of Apalachee" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Apalachee Nation · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Apalachee_Nation_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The historical Apalachee were a people who spoke a Muskogean language and lived on the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> until the beginning of the 18th century. English and Native slaving raids from the Carolina province between 1702 and 1704 drove refugees west from the Spanish missions, and some settled near the Mobilians at Mobile with the Tawasa and Chato. A yellow fever epidemic soon struck the newcomers. They were devout Catholics, and the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, Le Moyne de Bienville, noted both their religious zeal and their skill as workers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Apalachee later asked to move into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, and Director General Jean-Jacques Blaise d'Abbadie judged them good farmers and hunters who could help supply <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-orleans">New Orleans</a>. Roughly 80 survivors reached New Orleans in late September 1763 and went to settle on the Red River under their newly elected chief, Martin. In the 19th century their land there was sold away or taken; the planter Isaac Baldwin destroyed their homes and crops and used his slaves to drive them out. An appeal to Congress for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> in 1834 failed, and they moved to Emmanuel and Bayou Cypre.</p><p>Through the 20th century the community faced harassment from the Ku Klux Klan and the racism of the Jim Crow South, and many hid their Indigenous heritage. Only in the 1980s did the band's leaders feel secure enough in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a> to assert their identity in public, and in 1996 the Talimali Band of Apalachee Indians organised as a nonprofit based in Pineville, Louisiana.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The band, formerly called the Apalachee Indians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana">Louisiana</a>, has about 300 members and remains Catholic, and it took part in the Native American Mass at St. Francis Xavier Cathedral with other Louisiana tribes. It is neither federally nor state recognised. It submitted a letter of intent to petition for federal recognition in 1996 and an application on 26 August 1997, but no completed petition. Authorities in Leon County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, have recognised it as descended from the 18th-century Apalachee of Florida, and Louisiana admits its members to the scholarship programme of the state's Office of Indian Affairs without granting tribal recognition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Talimali Band of Apalachee Indians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Descendants survive in Louisiana as the <b>Talimali Band of Apalachee</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/apalachee-chiefdom">Apalachee chiefdom</a></li></ul></section>
